                PROBLEM DESCRITION
# =================================================== #


Table: books

+-------------+---------+
| Column Name | Type    |
+-------------+---------+
| book_id     | int     |
| title       | varchar |
| author      | varchar |
| genre       | varchar |
| pages       | int     |
+-------------+---------+

book_id is the unique ID for this table.

Each row contains information about a book including its genre and page count.


Table: reading_sessions

+----------------+---------+
| Column Name    | Type    |
+----------------+---------+
| session_id     | int     |
| book_id        | int     |
| reader_name    | varchar |
| pages_read     | int     |
| session_rating | int     |
+----------------+---------+

session_id is the unique ID for this table.

Each row represents a reading session where someone read a portion of a book. session_rating is on a scale of 1-5.

Write a solution to find books that have polarized opinions - books that receive both very high ratings and very low ratings from different readers.

• A book has polarized opinions if it has at least one rating ≥ 4 and at least one rating ≤ 2

• Only consider books that have at least 5 reading sessions

• Calculate the rating spread as (highest_rating - lowest_rating)

• Calculate the polarization score as the number of extreme ratings (ratings ≤ 2 or ≥ 4) divided by total sessions

• Only include books where polarization score ≥ 0.6 (at least 60% extreme ratings)

Return the result table ordered by polarization score in descending order, then by title in descending order.


# =================================================== #
                      SOLUTION
# =================================================== #


import pandas as pd

def find_polarized_books(books: pd.DataFrame, reading_sessions: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:
    
    reading_sessions['high_rating'] = reading_sessions['session_rating'] >= 4
    reading_sessions['low_rating'] = reading_sessions['session_rating'] <= 2
    reading_sessions['extreme_rating'] = reading_sessions['high_rating'] | reading_sessions['low_rating']

    session_data = (
        reading_sessions
        .groupby(by = 'book_id')
        .agg(
            total_sessions = ('session_id' , 'nunique'),
            max_rating = ('session_rating', 'max'),
            min_rating = ('session_rating', 'min'), 
            total_extreme_rating = ('extreme_rating', 'sum'),
            is_high_rated = ('high_rating', 'any'),
            is_low_rated = ('low_rating', 'any')
        )
        .reset_index()
    )

    session_data['rating_spread'] = session_data['max_rating'] - session_data['min_rating']
    session_data['polarization_score'] = (session_data['total_extreme_rating'] / session_data['total_sessions'] + 1e-6).round(decimals = 2)

    polarized_book_mask = (session_data['is_high_rated'] & session_data['is_low_rated'])
    polarization_score_mask = (session_data['polarization_score'] >= 0.6)
    session_mask = (session_data['total_sessions'] >= 5) 

    final_mask = polarized_book_mask & polarization_score_mask & session_mask

    result = pd.merge(
        left = books,
        right = session_data.loc[final_mask, ['book_id', 'rating_spread', 'polarization_score']],
        how = 'inner',
        on = 'book_id'
    )

    return result.sort_values(by = ['polarization_score', 'title'], ascending = [False, False])
